# Ask vs implement

Applies to **all** work in this repo (`frontend/`, `backend/`, `.cursor/`, etc.).

## Answer only (no code changes)

When the user is **asking**, not **ordering** work:

- Opinions, architecture, “what do you think”, “чи варто”, “як краще”, comparisons, explanations.
- Questions about existing code (“how does X work”, “what’s wrong with…”).
- Hypotheticals (“would it be better if…”) without an explicit request to change the repo.

**Do:** reply in chat (analysis, recommendation, tradeoffs).

**Do not:** edit files, run refactors, create commits, or “helpfully” apply the idea you just described — unless they clearly ask you to in the same message or a follow-up.

`@file` / open tabs / attached selections are **context**, not a command to implement.

## Implement when asked

Treat it as a **task** when the user clearly wants execution, e.g.:

- Imperatives: do it, make, add, fix, rename, move, refactor, apply, implement, create, update, wire up.
- Ukrainian equivalents: зроби, додай, виправ, перенеси, перейменуй, застосуй, реалізуй, напиши (code).

If intent is mixed (“what do you think — and if yes, do it”), follow the **action** part.

## If unclear

Prefer **one short clarifying question** or **answer-only** when the message reads as discussion. Do not start a large diff “just in case”.

## Exceptions

- User explicitly says to revert or undo something they did not want.
- A dedicated skill or agent workflow says to execute (e.g. `/reorganize-page-components` on a named folder).
- Trivial read-only commands needed **only** to answer the question (e.g. `grep` to cite code) are fine; still no file edits.
